# BUGS

The board's clock has been read only through the chip's documented registers; the driver has not been run on the board, and the register layout, the reset and VLF sequence for setting, and the bit positions of the flag and control registers are the chip's application manual's as the driver's author knew them, not yet checked against the manual.
The year ends at 2099: a later date can't be written.
The clock keeps no time zone, leap seconds or daylight saving; UTC is by convention.
A reading with VLF set gives no date until the clock is set, so the time service finds none on a board whose clock lost power.
The 12-hour mode isn't read: a clock set to it, by firmware, reads as the wrong hour.
The alarm, the timer, the update interrupt, the temperature compensation and the backup power register aren't driven.
